examples=[
('Two ways to decide','A traffic-light timer changes after a fixed time: the person wrote the timing rule. A classifier shown labelled shapes predicts a label for a new shape: the prediction comes from patterns in its training examples.'),
('An unusual input','A rule says IF the card is a square, put it in Box A. A new triangle may have no instruction. A learned pattern may also misclassify a new triangle if the examples were too narrow. Both failures need evidence.'),
('Choose by mechanism','A wheeled vehicle follows a stored turn sequence. It is following fixed rules. A separate model learns labels from examples of pieces. Calling both “robots” does not explain how their decisions are made.'),
('Separate the sets','Prepare eight short-piece examples and eight long-piece examples. Keep three fresh examples of each class hidden. Train on the sixteen training examples; then predict and test the six unseen examples. Do not move the test cards into training before the first test.'),
('From vague to checkable','Vague: “Help build a bridge.” Structured: “Goal: write assembly steps. Context: use this inventory and approved sketch for a 20 cm gap. Format: at most six short steps and three checks.” Then check whether each condition was met.'),
('Compare the same task','Both prompts must use the same inventory, match the same sketch and include three checks. Prompt A gives numbered steps; Prompt B gives a step/material/check table. Compare completion and accuracy rather than choosing the longest output.'),
('Three verdicts','Reference: the inventory has six plates and no cardboard. “Use six plates” matches that fact. “Cut a cardboard deck” conflicts with it. “Join it over there” is unclear because the connection and location are unspecified.'),
('Match check to output','For “use eight plates”, count the real plates. For “join the beam there”, compare the labelled sketch and ask which connection is meant. Rewriting the wording cannot replace checking the actual materials.'),
('Risk → person → rule','Risk: the guide invents a piece. Effect: the builder cannot finish and may waste time. Rule: compare every named piece and count with the inventory before using the guide. This reduces the risk but does not prove the structure will hold.'),
('Audit a fictional record','A sample log says: “A guide was used without comparing it with the pieces.” The gap is missing verification. Decide the likelihood and impact using evidence, then propose an inventory check. In class, use anonymised records where available.'),
('Connect the checks','A prompt-based guide uses an existing AI system. The prompt supplies facts and format. The output names an absent piece, so the accuracy verdict is incorrect. The responsible action is to reject that step and ask a person, not continue building.'),
('A complete use decision','“I will use this guide with changes. Its material list matches the tray, but Step 3 does not identify the joint. I will ask my teacher to confirm the connection and log the correction.” This combines accuracy, risk and an action.'),
('Five-part plan','Goal: a usable building guide. Inputs: inventory, dimensions and approved sketch. Structure: role, task, rule and format. Evaluation: compare pieces, sketch and test conditions. Risk: invented connections; mitigation: teacher verification and fallback.'),
('A pressure test','Normal input gives a clear gap, inventory and sketch. Tricky input gives “20 cm and 30 cm” for the same required gap. The expected action is ASK TEACHER and identify the conflict. If the output chooses silently, log the failure and improve validation.'),
('An evidence-based change','Version 1 names “a long beam” without identifying it. The checker cannot choose a piece. Version 2 requires each step to name a piece from the labelled inventory. Retest to see whether the output now identifies it correctly.'),
('Count accuracy honestly','If four of six requests satisfy every predetermined check, record 4/6. A neat answer does not count as correct when it invents a material. Keep the failing case and demonstrate the fallback before logging the fix.'),
('A useful peer comment','“Your first step names the piece clearly. Step 3 still says ‘attach here’; label that connection on the sketch.” The designer changes one feature and checks the new output against the same evidence.'),
('Check for regression','Keep cases 1–5 and their expected checks unchanged. A revision may improve missing-input handling but make a valid case fail. Record both improvements and regressions. A before-and-after table is stronger evidence than “it is better”.'),
('Justify two choices','“I used labelled input slots because the first output omitted the gap. I tested a conflicting-gap case because a normal case alone did not reveal validation problems.” Both choices name evidence and a reason.'),
('Specific feedback','Star tagged CE: “You checked all six outputs against stated criteria.” Wish tagged GE: “Explain who is affected by an invented connection and show the fallback.” A next goal could target independent verification rather than a bigger display.')
]
for b,(title,body) in zip(base,examples):b['example']={'title':title,'body':body}
material_sets={
1:[('Two comparison scenarios','A vending machine follows a stored price/change rule. A shape model studies labelled examples and predicts the label for a new drawing. Compare these cards if your teacher is not using video clips.'),('Three sorting cards','1 Traffic-light timer: its person-set timing never changes from examples. 2 Learning spam filter: trained on labelled messages. 3 Learning suggestion tool: trained on examples of text. Sort each and give a reason; these descriptions specify the mechanism.')],
2:[('Station A fixed-rule machine','Rule: IF a card has 3 sides, send it to A. IF it has 4 sides, send it to B. Otherwise send it to CHECK. Inputs: triangle, square, rectangle, a different triangle, and a circle. Record the result of every input.'),('Station B example-learning cards','Training cards: two small triangles labelled A, two large triangles labelled A, two squares labelled B and two rectangles labelled B. Infer a pattern from the examples. New cards: thin triangle, wide triangle, rotated square, long rectangle and circle. The teacher’s reference is A for 3-sided shapes, B for 4-sided shapes and CHECK otherwise. Predict before revealing/checking labels.'),('Compare','Did the rule machine change its rules? Which information did you infer from examples? What happened to the unusual circle? Log at least three differences, not just the labels.')],
3:[('Mechanism cards','Card A: a model learns piece labels from drawings. Card B: a robot repeats a stored turn sequence. Card C: a timer follows fixed intervals. Explain each mechanism; do not classify solely by the word robot or AI.'),('Word bank','Rule-based: person-written steps. Data: examples or information. Learning system: derives a pattern from examples. Model: the learned pattern used for a prediction.')],
4:[('Prepare the class sets','Use 8–10 examples per category, such as short/long pieces or circles/triangles. Hold back three different examples per category. All examples are non-personal objects/drawings. Label consistently and keep test cards out of training.'),('On-screen alternative','Open Workbench → Train a small model. It supplies eight examples per class and six held-back test cases. Compare the sets using the same test cases. It is a transparent numeric model, not a camera-based classifier.')],
5:[('A fictional practice task','Goal: create short instructions for a tabletop model. Sample inventory: 6 plates, 8 bricks and one 25 × 10 cm cardboard strip. Gap: 20 cm. Vehicle width: 5 cm. Sample sketch: two supports, one deck resting on both supports. Joining and fit need teacher verification; these facts are a prompt exercise, not a validated construction design.'),('Compare prompts','Vague: “Build a good bridge.” Structured: “Using the supplied inventory and sketch, give at most six short assembly steps, followed by inventory, gap/vehicle-fit and physical-test checks.” Compare actual completion against the task.')],
6:[('Three requirements for the richer task','1 Use only the stated inventory and approved sketch. 2 Use the requested short step format. 3 Include inventory, gap/vehicle-fit and physical-crossing checks. Keep the same task facts for both prompt designs.'),('Two structures to test','A: Goal → Context → Format, with numbered steps. B: Context → Goal → Format, with a step/material/check table. Run through your teacher or compare the clearly labelled fixed outputs in Workbench → Prompt builder. Verify each result; fixed samples do not react to your prompt.')],
7:[('Reference and outputs','Reference: 6 plates and no cardboard. Output 1: “Use six plates and compare the layout with the approved sketch.” Output 2: “Use eight plates and add cardboard.” Output 3: “Join it over there.” Decide correct/incorrect/unclear, give a reason and select a check. Correct here means the statement matches its reference; it does not prove the whole bridge works.')],
8:[('Four clinic stations','Open Workbench → Output triage. Station 1 has a count mismatch; Station 2 matches the inventory; Station 3 leaves a joint unclear; Station 4 makes an unsupported strength claim. Classify all four, choose verification actions and then defend your trust ladder.'),('Possible verification actions','Count the actual pieces. Compare with the approved sketch. Ask a person to identify the joint. Run a physical test before making a strength claim. Choose an action appropriate to the problem.')],
9:[('Risk cards','A: A guide invents a piece. B: It guarantees strength without a test. C: It requests a real learner’s full name and student number. D: Long instructions prevent a peer from accessing the task. Name affected user types and practical mitigations.'),('Rewrite the identifying input','Problem prompt: “Use my real name and real student number in every instruction.” Rewrite it with a fictional builder code or no identifier. Never enter actual identifying details into the site or a teacher-run AI tool.')],
10:[('Fictional audit log for practice','Entry 1: team used a guide without inventory verification. Entry 2: prompt used a fictional material list and the output was checked. Entry 3: a vague joint instruction was followed without asking. Entry 4: an identifying learner detail was requested; the teacher removed it before any tool use. Use anonymised actual class records instead when available.'),('Rule set to audit','Only teacher-operated AI. No personal data. Check outputs against references. Stop and ask a person when unclear. Be honest about limits. For each entry: identify the rule, gap, affected user and mitigation. Rank likelihood and impact with a reason.')],
11:[('Guided integrated case','Reference: a prompt-based guide must use 6 plates and no cardboard. Output: “Use 8 plates and a cardboard deck; it will definitely hold.” Name the system/task, build a structured prompt, give an accuracy verdict and a risk verdict, then justify use/change/reject.')],
12:[('The four station cards','Station 1: Which system mechanism is involved and why? Station 2: Write a Goal–Context–Format request using the fictional inventory from Lesson 5. Station 3: compare the output “use 8 plates” with a 6-plate inventory. Station 4: name the risk, affected user and mitigation. Finish with the use decision and weakest-strand diagnosis.')],
13:[('Five planning panels','1 Goal and user need. 2 Role/task/rule and prompt format. 3 Inputs: actual inventory, dimensions and approved sketch. 4 How output accuracy and usefulness will be checked. 5 Risk mitigation and human fallback. Submit to the teacher before building.'),('Practical brief','Teacher trials and agrees a manageable tabletop gap and the same toy vehicle before confirming the brief. Suggested starting point: 20 cm and three crossings. Use actual pieces and dimensions; do not assume the fictional practice inventory matches your kit.')],
14:[('Normal input card','A complete, teacher-approved inventory, one agreed gap, vehicle dimensions and labelled sketch. Define the correct-output checks before running.'),('Tricky input cards','A: gap missing. B: the same field says both 20 cm and 30 cm. C: the approved sketch needs cardboard, but the inventory excludes it. The expected response identifies the missing/conflicting fact and asks a person. Log the actual output and classified failure, not just the expected answer.')],
15:[('Build and version log','Version / input / actual output / evidence check / incorrect or unclear flag / change / result. Save both versions. Use actual teacher-run outputs or label curated responses as simulated.'),('Fixed samples for practice','Workbench → Prompt builder contains a weak output and a revised output for a fixed fictional inventory. They help you practise checking; they do not demonstrate that your own prompt has worked.')],
16:[('Six curated input cases','1 Complete approved task and real inventory. 2 Complete task with a different teacher-approved gap/sketch. 3 Only tape available, no structural pieces. 4 Required gap missing. 5 Two conflicting gaps. 6 Sketch needs cardboard, inventory excludes it.'),('Expected checks','Cases 1–2: respect all supplied facts/materials, use at most six steps, include all three final checks, and have human-checked feasible connections. Cases 3–6: ASK TEACHER and identify the specific problem. Count correct only if all stated checks are met. No generated guide is physically validated until tested.'),('Six-row log','For every case record input, actual output, expected check, verdict and reason. Then count correct/6, show a failure/fallback/fix and complete accuracy, clarity and privacy checks.')],
17:[('Review headings','Accuracy: matches the reference and constraints. Usefulness: helps the intended builder take the next action. Risk: identifies who could be affected and how harm/confusion is reduced. Feedback: two specific strengths and one actionable improvement.')],
18:[('Retest protocol','Choose at least five known cases from Lesson 16. Preserve the same requests, expected checks and conditions for before/after comparison. Keep failures and regressions visible.'),('Risk review prompts','Wrong output: what would the user do? Fairness/access: who might be left out? Privacy: are all inputs non-personal? Place risks on the matrix, act on any priority risk, confirm a person can intervene and state limitations honestly.')],
19:[('Classroom four-strand performance task','This is an adapted teacher-assessed task. Use the fictional reference: 6 plates, no cardboard, an approved sketch and a stated gap. Output: “use 8 plates and a cardboard deck; it will definitely hold.” Explain a fixed-rule versus learned decision, draft a structured request, evaluate the output with a reason/action, and identify a risk and mitigation. Your teacher may use the issued assessment instead.'),('Pitch and reflection','Present the finished solution and two justified design/evaluation choices using your own evidence. Ask/answer one why question, state one learning and set a next-stage goal.')],
20:[('Feedback slip','Station code [fictional]. Strand CU / SD / CE / GE. Star 1 with evidence ___. Star 2 with evidence ___. Wish with a practical next action ___. Visit at least two other stations.'),('Four-strand summary','CU: explain rule-based versus learning. SD: build a structured prompt solution. CE: verify outputs and choose use/change/reject. GE: identify affected users, risks and mitigations. Add your own evidence reference and next-stage goal.')]
}
